# Line endings.
#
# This file exists because of a real failure. Every .bat in this repo was
# authored on Linux and therefore had Unix (LF) line endings. cmd.exe parses
# batch files by byte offset, and with LF-only endings it loses its place and
# eats the first characters of lines. The user sees this:
#
#     'tle' is not recognized...          <- "title" lost "ti"
#     'tlocal' is not recognized...       <- "setlocal" lost "se"
#     'ho' is not recognized...           <- "echo" lost "ec"
#     [X] Python not found on PATH.       <- ...even though Python IS on PATH
#
# which reads as "the launcher is broken" and is impossible to diagnose from
# the error text. Batch files MUST ship with CRLF.
#
# `-text` (rather than `text eol=crlf`) means git stores and checks out these
# bytes EXACTLY as committed, with no normalisation in either direction. That
# matters because `text eol=crlf` only converts on clone — anyone downloading
# the ZIP from GitHub, or a single raw file, would still get LF and hit the
# same wall. The cost is that git shows .bat diffs as binary. Worth it.
*.bat   -text
*.cmd   -text
*.ps1   -text

# Shell scripts must stay LF or bash fails with "\r: command not found".
*.sh    text eol=lf

# Everything else: normalise to LF in the repo, leave checkout to the platform.
*.py    text eol=lf
*.js    text
*.jsx   text
*.json  text
*.md    text
*.txt   text
